course: dictionary
lesson: 3861
title: Fractions: parts of a whole
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A fraction is a part of a whole, such as one half or three quarters. The lesson's own example says: «A fraction like one third means: split the whole set into equal parts and take one part.»
explain: Fractions are written with one number over another, like 3/4. The bottom number (the denominator) tells how many equal parts the whole has. The top number (the numerator) tells how many of those parts we take.
explain: A fraction can also mean a very small part of something. «All of that takes a fraction of a second.» Here, a fraction of a second means only a tiny bit of time, not a whole second.
fact: A fraction is a part of a whole, such as one half or three quarters.
fact: The bottom number (denominator) tells how many equal parts the whole has.
fact: The top number (numerator) tells how many of those equal parts we take.
fact: «A fraction of a second» means a very small part of a second.
fact: «Some problems need two steps: first the area of a rectangle, then a fraction of that area.»
exercise: «A fraction like one third means: split the whole set into equal parts and take one part.» For one third, how many parts do we take? | Two | Four | Three | *One
exercise: In the fraction 3/4, which number is the bottom number, the denominator? | 1 | 3 | 7 | *4
exercise: «A chameleon's tongue shoots out in a fraction of a second.» What does a fraction of a second mean here? | An hour | *A very small part of a second | A whole minute | Exactly one whole second
test: In 3/4, what does the top number 3 tell us? | *How many of those parts we take | The number of wholes | How many equal parts the whole has | Whether the fraction is big
test: «Streaming services are reported to pay roughly a fraction of a penny per play.» What does a fraction of a penny mean? | *A very small part of a penny | A whole penny | Ten pennies | One pound
exam: Sara cuts a pizza into 4 equal slices and eats 1 slice. Which fraction of the pizza did she eat? | *1/4 | 4/4 | 1/2 | 4/1
